# Unity.Collections over Managed Allocation

For bulk data buffers, prefer `Unity.Collections` (`NativeArray<T>`, `NativeList<T>`, `NativeHashMap<K,V>`) over `new T[]` / `List<T>` when the data is large, processed in tight loops, or interfaces with jobs/physics/mesh APIs. Native containers live outside the GC heap — they never contribute to GC pressure or collection pauses.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — managed array allocated per rebuild, feeds the GC
private void RebuildMesh()
{
    var vertices = new Vector3[_count * 4];
    ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — native buffer, allocated once, disposed with the owner
private NativeArray<float3> _vertices;

private void Awake()
{
    _vertices = new NativeArray<float3>(MaxVertices, Allocator.Persistent);
}

private void OnDestroy()
{
    if (_vertices.IsCreated)
    {
        _vertices.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Allocator rules

| Allocator | Lifetime | Use for |
|-----------|----------|---------|
| `Allocator.Temp` | Current frame; auto-freed | Scratch buffers inside one method — cheapest; still call `Dispose` for clarity |
| `Allocator.TempJob` | ≤ 4 frames | Data passed into a job |
| `Allocator.Persistent` | Manual | Long-lived buffers — **must** have a `Dispose` in `OnDestroy`/teardown |

- Every `Persistent` container has exactly one owner responsible for `Dispose`. Guard with `IsCreated`.
- Never store a `Temp` container in a field — it dies at end of frame.
- Prefer `RaycastCommand`/`GetRawTextureData`/`Mesh.SetVertexBufferData`-style APIs that accept native containers directly, avoiding managed copies.

## When managed `List<T>` is still right

- Small collections (dozens of items), UI lists, infrequently touched data.
- Collections of class references (native containers require unmanaged element types).

Then follow allocation discipline: allocate once, pre-size, reuse with `Clear()`.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Bulk/hot buffers use `NativeArray`/`NativeList` with an explicit allocator
- [ ] Every `Persistent` container disposed in `OnDestroy`/teardown with `IsCreated` guard
- [ ] No `Temp` containers stored in fields
- [ ] Small/reference-typed collections stay managed but pre-sized and reused
